notes               = "Untitled in source. The date line is an event rather than a date: زلزله رودبار ۱۳۷۰, 'the Rudbar earthquake, 1370'. The Rudbar-Manjil earthquake actually struck on 31 Khordad ۱۳۶۹ (21 June 1990) and killed some 40,000 people, so the year printed here is one out from the event it names — transcribed as printed, not emended, and the Gregorian half is taken from the printed ۱۳۷۰. Imported as a draft with machine / lantern layers; ===translation=== left for Ben."
draft               = true

===persian===

در شبِ زلزله...
ماه در چشمِ کرمِ شب‌تاب
بارانی از مهتاب شد...
و... بر آوارها ریخت...
کرم شب‌تاب توان نداشت تا بشنود
غم‌انگیزترین هم‌سرایی را...
از مردمی که هرگز...
چنین یک دل با هم نخوانده‌اند...
تا... آخرین نفس...
زلزله رودبار ۱۳۷۰

===machine===

On the night of the earthquake...
the moon, in the eye of the firefly,
turned to a rain of moonlight...
and... poured down over the rubble...
The firefly had not the strength to hear
the most grief-stricken chorus of all...
from a people who had never...
sung so much of one heart together...
until... the last breath...
The Rudbar earthquake, 1370

===lantern===

On the night of the earthquake
the moon, in the eye of the firefly,
turned into a rain of moonlight
and poured down over the rubble...
The firefly had not the strength to hear
the most grief-stricken chorus of all —
from a people who had never
sung so much of one heart together
until the last breath...

The Rudbar earthquake, 1370

===translation===

===footnotes===

The Rudbar-Manjil earthquake destroyed a stretch of Gilan and Zanjan in the small hours of
31 Khordad ۱۳۶۹ — 21 June 1990 — and killed something like forty thousand people. The poem's own
date line says ۱۳۷۰, a year later than the event; whether that is the poet dating the writing or a
slip in the printing, the book does not say.

The chorus is the sound of the buried and the digging, heard as singing — a people who had never
been so much of one voice, and are so only now.
